{"session_id":"f4a72df9-5dd6-4e2e-8b37-470046177ca8","transcript_path":"/Users/ryan/.claude/projects/-Users-ryan-Documents-agent-marketing/f4a72df9-5dd6-4e2e-8b37-470046177ca8.jsonl","cwd":"/Users/ryan/Documents/agent_marketing","permission_mode":"acceptEdits","hook_event_name":"PostToolUse","tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"git -C /Users/ryan/Documents/agent_marketing add app/controllers/scout_controller.rb && git commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nFix missing RAG source indicator in Scout chat responses\n\nProblem: When users ask Scout questions using uploaded documents, the source \nattribution badge was not showing, even though the read_document tool was being \ncalled successfully.\n\nRoot cause: Scout controller was not extracting and returning source information \nfrom tools_used array in the response. The frontend was ready to display sources \nvia renderSourceBadges(), but the controller never sent the data.\n\nSolution:\n1. Add extract_response_sources() helper method to ScoutController\n   - Inspects tools_used array for read_document tool calls\n   - Builds sources array with format expected by frontend\n   - Returns { type: 'documents', count: 1 } for each document read\n\n2. Include sources in JSON response\n   - Sources array now sent to frontend in chat response\n   - Frontend renderSourceBadges() will display \"Documents\" badge\n   - Properly indicates when response is based on uploaded documents\n\nTesting:\n- Upload insurance document via Scout\n- Ask question about the document content\n- Should now see \"Documents\" badge below the response\n\nFiles modified:\n- app/controllers/scout_controller.rb\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\n)\"","description":"Commit RAG source indicator fixes"},"tool_response":{"stdout":"[dev e79e931] Fix missing RAG source indicator in Scout chat responses\n Committer: Ryan Martin <ryan@Ryans-Mac-mini.local>\nYour name and email address were configured automatically based\non your username and hostname. Please check that they are accurate.\nYou can suppress this message by setting them explicitly:\n\n    git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\n    git config --global user.email you@example.com\n\nAfter doing this, you may fix the identity used for this commit with:\n\n    git commit --amend --reset-author\n\n 1 file changed, 60 insertions(+)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
